  <title>Arithmetic in quantity cells</title>

  <p>A quantity cell takes a sum as well as a number. Type the sum and press
  <key>Enter</key> or <key>Tab</key>: the cell shows the result, and the result
  is what is saved. The sum itself is not kept.</p>

  <terms>
    <item><title><input>2x12</input></title><p>24</p></item>
    <item><title><input>3*12+4</input></title><p>40</p></item>
    <item><title><input>(3+4)*6</input></title><p>42</p></item>
    <item><title><input>10/4</input></title><p>2.5</p></item>
    <item><title><input>=2*3</input></title><p>6</p></item>
  </terms>

  <list>
    <item><p><key>+</key> adds and <key>-</key> subtracts. <key>*</key>,
    <key>x</key> and <key>×</key> multiply. <key>/</key> and <key>÷</key>
    divide.</p></item>
    <item><p>Multiplying and dividing come before adding and subtracting, so
    <input>2+3*4</input> is 14. Use brackets to change the order.</p></item>
    <item><p>A leading <key>=</key> is allowed, as in a spreadsheet.</p></item>
    <item><p>Numbers are plain digits with an optional decimal point. Leave out
    commas: <input>1,000</input> is refused, so type <input>1000</input>.</p>
    </item>
  </list>

  <p>The result is rounded to the places the window keeps. An invoice keeps two
  decimal places, so <input>10/3</input> is saved as 3.33. A credit memo keeps
  one, and a purchase order only whole units. Cells that only hold whole
  numbers refuse a sum that comes out as a fraction. These are an invoice line
  for a product counted in inventory or serial numbered, an inventory count, an
  inventory transfer, a manufacturing project's quantities, an assembled
  product's parts, a resource and a vendor's buy quantity.</p>

  <p>A sum that cannot be worked out, such as <input>2*</input>, a word, or a
  division by zero, leaves the quantity as it was. Most windows also say why.</p>

</page>
